ns, cover letters, tracker, more roles Driven by customer use and sustainable operating costsThe initial role set should include web development, photography, sales, and marketing, plus a limited selection validated for the first market.How the business earns without reducing customer valueKeep the core starting tools useful for free. Charge for clearly defined additional work:Personal resume review.Targeted rewriting.Application packs.Optional advanced assistance with transparent limits.Price human work after measuring editing time, support, revisions, payment fees, and AI costs. I would not promise unlimited human work at the current prices without testing whether it is sustainable.My recommendation: build a calm, role-aware resume platform with honest checks and genuinely useful downloads. That is a stronger foundation than a loud sales page promising to “beat ATS.”Completed now: the typography-only logo.Not yet implemented: the website redesign, templates, builder, or backend.Still unverified: current search-demand rankings and vendor-specific ATS documentation.Would you like this revised plan put into a Word document or PDF?before tha reanalyse with these new resources that can make the site more worthfulhttps://github.com/topics/applicant-tracking-systemhttps://github.com/srbhr/resume-matcherhttps://github.com/topics/resume-makerhttps://github.com/sunnypatell/ats-screenerhttps://github.com/devxprite/resumaveRead and analyse how these repos can make difference in our plan to provide ultimate services to our customers or visitors. And can we applied them in my site as tool to use for the ebst output, and of course it must be organize to set them in the sitees—but use them as design references and selected building blocks, not as three whole applications pasted into ATSresumeI reviewed the repositories’ current public descriptions, metadata, and relevant README/source material on 21 October 2026. I haven’t installed or run the full projects, so this is a product and integration assessment—not a complete code, security, or ATS-vendor validation.What each repository could contributeTableRepository Useful ideas for ATSresume What I would not copy blindlyResume Matcher A journey from a master resume to a job-specific version; job-description comparison; cover letters; interview preparation; reusable templates and PDF export. This is the strongest reference for a broader career toolkit. It is a substantial self-hosted application, not a plug-in for your Vite/React site: its documented stack includes Next.js, FastAPI, a database, and a PDF-rendering service. I would borrow the product flow first, not port the whole system. The repository lists an Apache-2.0 license.ATS Screener File parsing, a readable score breakdown, job-description comparison, explainable suggestions, and separate parsing/scoring tests. Its documented flow parses a PDF or DOCX in the browser, then sends extracted text to an AI service for analysis. Its “six ATS” results are simulations, not verified scores from the actual employer systems. The README itself describes Greenhouse as not having automatic scoring by design, while the tool still produces a Greenhouse profile score. Its MIT license is listed by GitHub, but its code and profile assumptions still need careful review.Resumave A simpler guided builder, no-sign-up starting point, locally saved drafts, responsive layout, and PDF export—useful inspiration for making the first step easy. It documents A4 PDF export, not a full role-aware application workflow with DOCX and job tailoring. Its code was last pushed in 2024. Its README says MIT, but GitHub’s API did not detect a license and the LICENSE file could not be retrieved. I would not reuse its code until that discrepancy is resolved.The Applicant Tracking System and Resume Maker GitHub topic pages are useful places to discover other projects. They are not quality reviews, security checks, or endorsements.My recommendation: build one connected resume workspaceThe best outcome is not three separate checkers and builders. It is one resume workspace where the visitor’s information moves through a clear process:Choose a starting point: browse templates and examples by role and career stage.Build a master resume: enter experience once and keep an editable original.Tailor a copy to a job: paste a job description and compare it with the resume.Review the document: inspect extracted text and check structure, readability, and possible parsing issues.Download and apply: export usable documents and keep track of applications.Get human help if wanted: offer review or rewriting as an optional paid service.For launch, I’d organize the main navigation around Templates · Examples · Build a resume · Check against a job · Pricing. Later additions—cover letters, saved job-specific versions, and an application tracker—can fit into the same workspace rather than crowding the homepage.A visitor should be able to use the basic templates and builder without being forced to create an account or pay to see the result. An account can be optional for people who want cloud saving or application tracking.Important corrections to the current checkerThe code you shared already shows why a rewrite is worthwhile:atsScanner.ts estimates layout/readability partly from word count and the presence of contact details. With pasted text alone, it cannot inspect the original file’s columns, text boxes, or reading order.Its keyword check selects frequent terms from a job description, which can surface generic or irrelevant words.The page says the tool identifies “automated rejections,” predicts “75%+” filtering likelihood, and promotes “95+” scores. The code does not establish those outcomes.It presents a fixed “Google XYZ” formula and quantified-metric expectations as universal rules. Metrics should be used when they are truthful and relevant—not manufactured to meet a score.I would replace the prominent “ATS score” with clear findings such as:What we noticedEvidence from your resumeWhy it may matterA suggested next stepFor job matching, show whether each requirement is supported by the resume, present but unclear, or not evidenced in the supplied material. “Not evidenced” must never mean “add this skill anyway.”Even a well-tested checker cannot promise that a resume will pass every employer’s system, reach a recruiter, or secure an interview. ATS products and employer settings vary, and a job seeker’s application answers and qualifications matter too.What I would build first1. Templates and role guidanceStart with a small set of good templates, then adapt the content guidance for web developers, photographers, sales professionals, and marketers. A role guide should tell visitors what evidence is useful for that kind of work; it shouldn’t imply everyone in the role needs the same resume.2. Builder and downloadsUse one structured resume record to power the editor, preview, and exports. Provide editable DOCX, text-based PDF, and a plain-text option where practical. Preserve a master copy and let users create job-specific versions so tailoring never overwrites their original.Test the files customers will actually download: extract text from the export, verify section order and links, and check page breaks and clipping. A polished preview is not sufficient if the exported document is broken.3. Resume and job-description reviewUse the useful ideas from ATS Screener—file parsing, job-description comparison, and specific feedback—but describe the results as resume checks and job comparison, not official scores from Workday, Greenhouse, or another vendor.Make the limits visible. If a visitor pastes text, say that the original layout was not checked. If the visitor uploads a document, show what the parser extracted so they can catch errors.4. Optional career toolsAfter the core builder is dependable, add a cover-letter helper and saved application versions. An application tracker can come later. I would not make automatic mass-applying a launch feature: helping someone prepare a relevant, accurate application is a better starting point.Technical and privacy approachYour supplied site is already React and Vite. I would first build these as modules in that existing stack rather than transplanting Resume Matcher’s Next.js/FastAPI architecture or ATS Screener’s SvelteKit application.For privacy, a crucial distinction is: “The file stays in your browser” does not necessarily mean “your resume data stays in your browser.” ATS Screener’s README says that the document is parsed locally but its extracted text is sent to Gemini for analysis. ATSresume should disclose any external processing before it happens, name the type of data sent, explain retention, and give people a non-AI or local-processing option where feasible. Don’t send a resume to a model silently.Revised build orderTablePhase Deliverable1. Trust and foundation Remove unsupported score, rejection, and guarantee claims; define privacy and retention; establish one resume data model.2. Useful free start Publish a focused template library and role-specific examples; create the builder and test export quality.3. Honest checking Add file/text checks and job-description comparison with evidence and limitations stated in the result.4. Paid service Connect optional human review to genuine payment, intake, delivery, and revision workflows.5. Expand based on use Add cover letters, saved tailored versions, and an application tracker as customer feedback supports them.Reuse and verification caveatsResume Matcher: GitHub reports an Apache-2.0 license. If you reuse its code, review the license and attribution obligations, plus the licenses of its dependencies and models.ATS Screener: GitHub reports MIT. Its research documents, security notes, and implementation are useful references—but its platform-specific scoring remains the project’s own simulation, not official vendor behaviour.Resumave: the README’s MIT claim conflicts with the missing/undetected license information. Confirm the license before copying any of its code.Bottom line: take the career-workspace flow from Resume Matcher, the parsing and explainable-check pattern from ATS Screener, and the low-friction builder experience from Resumave. Build those ideas as one calm, minimalist ATSresume product—and be rigorous that the tool helps people improve their applications without claiming it can predict an employer’s decision. I haven’t changed the site yet.